WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.680
Chapter one followed a vesicle through three handoffs: bud, catch, fuse.

2
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:08.280
But how did anyone put those handoffs in order?

3
00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:11.480
What can a pile of stuck cargo tell you?

4
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:16.033
First, measure the block.

5
00:00:17.453 --> 00:00:19.433
Yeast secrete an enzyme called invertase.

6
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:25.393
The fraction secreted is the enzyme outside the cell, divided by all of it.

7
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:29.087
Is the trapped enzyme dead, or only waiting?

8
00:00:30.787 --> 00:00:34.647
Return the cells to 25 °C, with new protein synthesis blocked.

9
00:00:35.907 --> 00:00:39.907
If the trapped enzyme moves outside, it was waiting to be delivered.

10
00:00:41.017 --> 00:00:45.097
At 37 °C, these secretion-blocked cells become dense.

11
00:00:46.397 --> 00:00:51.057
In 1980, Novick, Field and Schekman used that to enrich new mutants.

12
00:00:52.617 --> 00:00:55.697
Electron microscopy then showed what each one piled up.

13
00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:01.990
Chapter one's rule applies here: on a linear route, cargo piles up in the last place

14
00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:07.610
it reached. In 1981, double mutants put the blocks in a broad order.

15
00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:10.070
Now a harder case.

16
00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:15.630
An enzyme called NSF resets SNAREs at many steps along the route.

17
00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:20.350
Where would a heat-sensitive NSF mutant pile up its cargo?

18
00:01:26.467 --> 00:01:28.207
At its earliest blocked step.

19
00:01:29.427 --> 00:01:36.227
In classical sec18 experiments, ER and small ER-derived vesicles piled up, not late

20
00:01:36.227 --> 00:01:37.267
secretory vesicles.

21
00:01:38.587 --> 00:01:42.867
A pile shows where transport stops, not when or where else a protein acts.

22
00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:45.950
Test tubes add what a pile cannot.

23
00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:52.190
Cell-free assays mix donor and acceptor membranes with cytosol and ATP.

24
00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:56.410
Then each step can be timed, removed or rebuilt.

25
00:01:57.533 --> 00:01:59.233
Is catching enough to fuse?

26
00:02:00.673 --> 00:02:04.933
Yeast HOPS is a tether that binds the Rab Ypt7.

27
00:02:06.333 --> 00:02:12.333
Separate forms missing the beta propeller of Vps11 or Vps18 still tethered

28
00:02:12.333 --> 00:02:14.313
Ypt7-bearing lipid spheres.

29
00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:17.153
But they failed to promote fusion.

30
00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:20.487
So read stuck cargo in two ways.

31
00:02:21.627 --> 00:02:25.427
Where cargo stops gives the order; test tubes split catch from fuse.
